---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---
<SiteLayout
  title="First Alarm"
  description="Least privilege, unexpected vendor destinations, log evidence, and why verification beats guessing."
>
  <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">null route queen / firewall notes / field lesson</p>

      <h1 id="article-title" class="page-title">First Alarm</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        A simple vendor-cloud firewall request stopped being simple the moment
        the traffic logs showed a destination nobody had mentioned.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="request">
      <h2 id="request">Start narrow</h2>

      <p>
        The request was straightforward: allow a fire panel to reach the vendor's
        cloud service, but only on the ports the application actually required.
      </p>

      <p>
        The policy was intentionally restrictive. The expected traffic was limited
        to ports <code>2001</code>, <code>4001</code>, and <code>6001</code>,
        with destination access kept as narrow as practical.
      </p>

      <p>
        Least privilege is not about making the rule difficult. It is about
        understanding exactly what the device needs and allowing no more than that.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="logs">
      <h2 id="logs">Then the logs told a different story</h2>

      <p>
        During testing, the panel tried to reach an additional destination that
        was not part of the original information.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is where it is easy to make one of two bad decisions:
        block it because it was unexpected, or widen the rule because the device
        was not working.
      </p>

      <p>
        Neither answer was justified yet. The logs had given us evidence of an
        attempted connection. They had not told us why the destination was needed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify">Verify before you widen</h2>

      <p>
        The next step was not to guess. We checked the destination against the
        vendor's requirements, and the vendor confirmed that it was legitimate
        for the service.
      </p>

      <p>
        That confirmation changed the situation. The new destination was no longer
        "mystery traffic." It was a documented dependency that could be evaluated
        and added on purpose.
      </p>

      <p>
        Weird does not automatically mean malicious. Weird means: go look.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="change">
      <h2 id="change">Make the smallest justified change</h2>

      <p>
        Once the destination was verified, the firewall policy could be adjusted
        without turning a tightly scoped rule into a broad exception.
      </p>

      <p>
        The change still followed the normal review process. A second set of eyes
        mattered because firewall work has a habit of looking obvious right before
        it becomes somebody else's outage.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal was not "make it work at any cost." The goal was:
        make it work, understand why it works, and preserve the control that made
        the rule safe in the first place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 id="lesson">The lesson</h2>

      <p>
        Least privilege and good customer service are not opposites.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not have to choose between reflexively saying no and opening the
        firewall wider than necessary. Investigate the evidence, verify the
        dependency, document what changed, and make the narrowest change that
        solves the real problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        Security works better when "no" is not the first tool in the box.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <p><a href="/firewall-notes/">← Back to Firewall Notes</a></p>
</SiteLayout>
